# UST.G3DL H02 Two-Prime Reconstruction Holdout Theorem v1

## Status

`CLOSED_EXACT_TWO_PRIME_RECONSTRUCTION_WITH_INDEPENDENT_THIRD_PRIME_HOLDOUT`

G3DI and G3DJ emit the normalized H02 kernel coefficients modulo 21,817 and
33,863. G3DK independently reserves the complete kernel modulo 37,493. G3DL
uses the third prime only after constructing every two-prime candidate.

## Reconstruction contract

For each of the 46,032,168 power-basis coefficients, G3DL first combines the
two construction residues modulo

```text
M2 = 21817 * 33863 = 738789071.
```

It applies extended-Euclidean rational reconstruction with

```text
|numerator| <= 19219,
1 <= denominator <= 19219,
2 * 19219^2 < M2.
```

This is the standard uniqueness range: there is at most one reduced fraction
inside the stated box for a given residue. Prime 37,493 is then consulted as a
strict out-of-sample test.

The exact result is:

```text
two-prime candidates:       42,352,640
third-prime holdout passes: 36,652,627
rejected CRT aliases:        5,700,013
no two-prime candidate:      3,679,528
```

Thus finite rank agreement did not justify accepting every two-prime
reconstruction. The reserved prime removes more than five million false
aliases before they can enter a purported characteristic-zero relation.

After admitting the third residue, reconstruction modulo

```text
M3 = 27,699,418,639,003
```

with uniqueness bound 3,721,519 emits 42,353,676 candidates. These candidates
are explicitly reserved for a fourth-prime holdout; they are not promoted.

## What Changed

The multiprime program now has a falsifiable coefficient-level protocol:
construct on earlier primes, test on an unseen prime, and only then expand the
CRT. It no longer treats cross-prime rank agreement as coefficient recovery.

All candidate and verdict masks are persisted in canonical
degree-row-column order, together with a digest of the candidate stream and
representative samples.

## Boundary

A fraction that passes one finite holdout is strong evidence, not an identity
over `Q(gamma)`. G3DL proves neither a global coefficient-height bound nor the
exact polynomial equation

```text
Q(gamma) N(gamma) = 0.
```

It therefore does not prove `rank_Q(gamma)(Q)=4748`, a characteristic-zero
second Woodbury factorization, the H02 source contraction, path integration,
integral marking, readout or `beta_C`.

The next certificate must execute a fourth split prime, test the three-prime
candidates out of sample, and continue toward either direct exact relation
verification or a sufficient height theorem.

